    "result_text": "Build passes. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Change\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` flow now emits an immutable structured `AUDIT` event alongside the existing audit line, as required.\n\n**New unit \u2014 `OwnerCreatedEvent`** (`model/OwnerCreatedEvent.java`): a small, cohesive final class that logs the JSON object `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:'OWNER_CREATED'}` to the `AUDIT` logger. `seq` comes from a static `AtomicLong`, giving a monotonically increasing integer across all creates. The event's identifier value is routed through a private `primaryId(Owner)` method that returns the customerCode today \u2014 the single place that will switch to the memberId later, so the event follows the primary identifier without touching anything else.\n\n**Wiring \u2014 one line in `OwnerAuditListener.onCreate`**: added `OwnerCreatedEvent.emit(owner);`.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- I deliberately avoided registering the emitter as a second `@EntityListeners` on `Owner` \u2014 that would have edited `Owner.java`, whose large `WMC_other` dominates the cost. Instead I wired through the tiny `OwnerAuditListener` (its only other method is a trivial `membershipNumber`, so `WMC_other \u2248 1`).\n- The only edited function, `onCreate`, is CC 1 with a 1-line change; all new behaviour lives in the new class, which starts with `WMC_other = 0`.\n- Two files touched total.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Emitted line observed: `{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`, carrying the owner id and the customerCode the test reads back.\n- `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
